        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "8b6ee281-139a-5210-83b6-7637e81147e4",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: Kolozsvár, Cluj, Romania, Hungary, Verőce\n- Events: World War I, World War II, Treaty of Trianon, Second Vienna Award\n- Organizations: Sixty-Four Counties Youth Movement\n- People: László Toroczkai\n- Projects: Kolozsvár Ghetto, Magyar Sziget festival\n- Concepts: Hungarian revisionism, unification of ethnic Hungarians\n\nFacts:\n- The Treaty of Trianon was the 1920 peace agreement that formally ended World War I between most Allied powers and the Kingdom of Hungary.\n- The Treaty of Trianon reduced Hungary to a landlocked state of 93,073 square kilometers, about 28% of the 325,411 square kilometers of the pre-war Kingdom of Hungary.\n- Between the 1920 Treaty of Trianon and the 1940 Second Vienna Award, Cluj was part of Romania.\n- The Kolozsvár Ghetto was one of the lesser-known Jewish ghettos of the World War II era.\n- The Sixty-Four Counties Youth Movement is a far-right movement in Hungary that advocates unifying ethnic Hungarians living outside Hungary and revising the 1920 Treaty of Trianon.\n- The Magyar Sziget festival is a nationalist festival in Verőce, Hungary, organized by the Sixty-Four Counties Youth Movement under the leadership of László Toroczkai.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "4fadb07c-31e5-55ec-9a9d-c978c96e7694",
        "text": "Page: Kolozsvár Ghetto. Sentence: The Kolozsvár Ghetto was one of the lesser-known Jewish ghettos of the World War II era.\nPage: Kolozsvár Ghetto. Sentence: Between the signing of the Treaty of Trianon in 1920 and the Second Vienna Award in 1940, Cluj was a part of Romania.\nPage: Treaty of Trianon. Sentence: The Treaty of Trianon was the peace agreement of 1920 to formally end World War I between most of the Allies of World War I and the Kingdom of Hungary, the latter being one of the successor states to Austria-Hungary.\nPage: Treaty of Trianon. Sentence: It left Hungary as a landlocked state covering 93073 sqkm , only 28% of the 325411 sqkm that had constituted the pre-war Kingdom of Hungary (the Hungarian half of the Austro-Hungarian monarchy).\nPage: Sixty-Four Counties Youth Movement. Sentence: The Sixty-Four Counties Youth Movement (Hungarian: \"Hatvannégy Vármegye Ifjúsági Mozgalom\" , HVIM) is a far-right movement in Hungary advocating the unification of all ethnic Hungarians that live outside of Hungary and the revision of the Treaty of Trianon from 1920, which defined the current borders of the Hungarian state.\nPage: Magyar Sziget. Sentence: The Magyar Sziget festival (\"Hungarian Island\") is a nationalist festival organised in the Hungarian city of Verőce, by the Sixty-Four Counties Youth Movement (HVIM) under leadership of László Toroczkai.",
